# KEUR-DOC-007 — README is too long (too-much / clutter → move detail to docs/).
# @implements ADR-061 (anti-pattern: "too much clutter"), APS RFC ("one page")
#
# max-lines engine: fires once when the file exceeds a line budget. The RFC and
# purposeful-readme agree a README is an entry point and a map, not a
# destination — "it should fit on one piece of paper", staccato. A bloated
# README is the clutter anti-pattern: the reader cannot find the quick start
# under the walls of detail that belong in docs/.
#
# Budget: 200 lines. Generous (keur's own README is ~400 and is a deliberate,
# content-rich public face — it will fire and should carry a keur:ignore or be
# split; that is the honest conversation this rule starts). WARN, never block.
id: KEUR-DOC-007
ilities: [analysability]
maturity: 3
title: README exceeds the one-page budget (move detail to docs/)
category: quality
severity: warning
engine: max-lines
action: warn
min_count: 200
target:
  filenames: README.md
patterns:
  - regex: ""
    message: "README exceeds ~200 lines. A README is a map, not a destination (ADR-061): keep a staccato quick start and move depth (architecture, extended usage, tutorials) to docs/ or companion files, linked. Clutter buries the one thing a newcomer needs."
confidence: 0.5
fix: "Move long sections (detailed configuration, architecture essays, extended examples) into docs/ and link them from a short 'More info' chapter. Keep the README to the six chapters at quick-start depth."
